# API Reporter mTLS Test – RCM 10.82.29.59 – Native PowerShell

## 1. Confirm the Windows user

Run the test under the same Windows user/account that owns the client certificate.

```powershell
whoami
```

---

## 2. Define the client certificate thumbprint

```powershell
$thumbprint = "474b0acd5369a1266d8a60b09474c860f7745aa0"
```

---

## 3. Load the certificate from the Current User certificate store

```powershell
$cert = Get-Item "Cert:\CurrentUser\My\$thumbprint"
```

---

## 4. Display the certificate

```powershell
$cert
```

Check that the Subject, Issuer and Thumbprint correspond to the expected T910212 client certificate.

---

## 5. Verify that the certificate has an associated private key

```powershell
$cert.HasPrivateKey
```

Expected result:

```text
True
```

If the result is `False`, stop the test because the certificate cannot be used for mTLS.

---

## 6. Display useful certificate information

```powershell
$cert.Subject
```

```powershell
$cert.Issuer
```

```powershell
$cert.Thumbprint
```

```powershell
$cert.NotBefore
```

```powershell
$cert.NotAfter
```

```powershell
$cert.HasPrivateKey
```

---

## 7. Temporarily bypass the VIP and map the hostname to RCM .59

PowerShell must be running with sufficient privileges to modify the Windows hosts file.

```powershell
Add-Content -Path "$env:SystemRoot\System32\drivers\etc\hosts" -Value "`n10.82.29.59 fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net"
```

This temporarily maps:

```text
fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net
        |
        +----> 10.82.29.59
```

instead of using the normal VIP.

---

## 8. Clear the Windows DNS cache

```powershell
Clear-DnsClientCache
```

---

## 9. Verify the hostname resolution

```powershell
Resolve-DnsName fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net
```

The test must resolve to:

```text
10.82.29.59
```

Do not continue if it still resolves to the VIP `10.82.15.221`.

---

## 10. Test TCP connectivity to the dedicated Reporter port

```powershell
Test-NetConnection fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net -Port 9443
```

Expected:

```text
RemoteAddress    : 10.82.29.59
RemotePort       : 9443
TcpTestSucceeded : True
```

---

## 11. Execute the mTLS API request

```powershell
$response = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net:9443/reporter/api/reporter" -Certificate $cert
```

This uses the certificate and its associated private key directly from the Windows Current User certificate store.

---

## 12. Check the HTTP status

```powershell
$response.StatusCode
```

Expected:

```text
200
```

---

## 13. Display the API Reporter JSON response

```powershell
$response.Content
```

Expected result:

```text
JSON returned by API Reporter
```

---

# Expected Successful Flow

```text
T910212 certificate
        |
        +-- HasPrivateKey = True
        |
        v
PowerShell Invoke-WebRequest
        |
        | HTTPS :9443
        | Client Certificate
        v
10.82.29.59:9443
        |
        v
Apache
        |
        | SSLVerifyClient require
        |
        +-- Certificate validation
        |
        +-- SSL_CLIENT_VERIFY = SUCCESS
        |
        v
ProxyPass /reporter
        |
        v
127.0.0.1:8000
        |
        v
API Reporter
        |
        v
HTTP 200 + JSON
```

---

## 14. IMPORTANT – Remove the temporary hosts-file entry after the test

First display the hosts file:

```powershell
Get-Content "$env:SystemRoot\System32\drivers\etc\hosts"
```

Remove the temporary line:

```text
10.82.29.59 fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net
```

Do not leave this mapping permanently because it bypasses the normal UAT VIP.

After removing the entry, clear the DNS cache:

```powershell
Clear-DnsClientCache
```

Verify that normal DNS resolution has been restored:

```powershell
Resolve-DnsName fcscmt-actone-uat-ch.appl.ubs.net
```

It should again resolve through the normal UAT infrastructure rather than being forced to `10.82.29.59`.

---

# Server-side verification

At the same time, on RCM `10.82.29.59`, monitor the dedicated Reporter SSL log:

```bash
tail -f logs/reporter_ssl.log
```

For a successful mTLS request we expect values similar to:

```text
VERIFY="SUCCESS"
CN="..."
SUBJECT="..."
ISSUER="..."
SERIAL="..."
```

The exact `CN` captured here should be used later for the final Apache CN authorization rule.
